1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,600
Brevio Studio turns one long lecture into a short, reviewed video, for the audience you choose.

2
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:11,181
Lectures hold great ideas, but few people watch an hour of video.

3
00:00:11,181 --> 00:00:16,450
Cutting highlights by hand is slow, and fully automatic tools are hard to trust.

4
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:22,737
You start with a lecture video and pick the audience: researchers, the general public, or kids.

5
00:00:22,737 --> 00:00:24,375
A source link is optional.

6
00:00:24,675 --> 00:00:30,187
Brevio transcribes the lecture on your own computer and suggests the strongest clips.

7
00:00:30,187 --> 00:00:32,975
You keep up to five and fine tune each one.

8
00:00:33,275 --> 00:00:39,030
Before writing anything, it plans the story: clip count, opening idea, bridges and ending.

9
00:00:39,030 --> 00:00:42,100
The finished video is checked against that plan.

10
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:51,700
It builds a preview with a narrated opening, bridges and ending, plus illustrations, music and captions, each section beside its source evidence.

11
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,300
Nothing is exported until a named reviewer has watched the preview and approved it.

12
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:07,000
Every export gets two plain-language checks: technical, for media, captions and loudness, and structure, for order, bridges, narration and the plan.

13
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:10,710
Transcription, voices and rendering run locally.

14
00:01:10,710 --> 00:01:15,895
Only short text requests use the company gateway, capped at five per run.

15
00:01:15,895 --> 00:01:17,600
A person always decides.

16
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:21,823
Faster highlights, consistent quality, and a human in control.

17
00:01:21,823 --> 00:01:25,050
Try it on one lecture, and tell us what to improve.
